# why: Quitting the browser could hang forever: a Wayland dispatch posted to a UI thread that has stopped.
With threaded polling (Ozone/Wayland with --in-process-gpu, as on webOS), the thread that watches the wl_display fd
dispatches events by posting WlDisplayDispatchPendingQueueInternal to the UI thread and blocking on a WaitableEvent
until it runs. At shutdown the UI thread's message loop ends first, then OzonePlatformWayland::PostMainMessageLoopRun
destroys WaylandConnection, and ~WaylandEventWatcherThread joins the watching thread. If an event arrived in between,
the posted task never runs: the watching thread waits for it and the UI thread waits for the join. On the LG C1 this
happened on menu > Exit (and when webOS closed the app), reproduced with the New Tab page and with Google Maps open,
not with a static page: the window went away and the chrome process stayed alive with both threads in futex_wait. Stacks taken
from /proc/<pid>/mem: main thread in Thread::Stop <- ~WaylandEventWatcherThread <- ~WaylandConnection <-
PostMainMessageLoopRun, "wayland-fd" thread in WaitableEvent::TimedWaitImpl <- WlDisplayDispatchPendingQueue.
The watcher now also waits on ui_thread_gone_ (manual reset, so every later dispatch passes too), which its
destructor signals on the UI thread before the join. The abandoned task still holds &event, which is gone by then:
safe, because the stopped UI loop never runs it and deleting the task only drops the pointer.

--- a/src/ui/events/platform/wayland/wayland_event_watcher.cc
+++ b/src/ui/events/platform/wayland/wayland_event_watcher.cc
@@ -142,6 +142,9 @@
 
 WaylandEventWatcher::~WaylandEventWatcher() {
   DCHECK(!watching_);
+  // The UI thread's message loop has ended, so a dispatch the watching thread
+  // posts to it will never run. Release that thread before |thread_| joins it.
+  ui_thread_gone_.Signal();
 }
 
 void WaylandEventWatcher::SetShutdownCb(
@@ -307,7 +310,10 @@
         &WaylandEventWatcher::WlDisplayDispatchPendingQueueInternal, weak_this_,
         &event);
     ui_thread_task_runner_->PostTask(FROM_HERE, std::move(cb));
-    event.Wait();
+    // Not event.Wait(): at shutdown the UI thread stops running tasks and then
+    // joins this thread in ~WaylandEventWatcher, and both would wait forever.
+    base::WaitableEvent* events[] = {&event, &ui_thread_gone_};
+    base::WaitableEvent::WaitMany(events);
   } else {
     WlDisplayDispatchPendingQueueInternal(nullptr);
   }
--- a/src/ui/events/platform/wayland/wayland_event_watcher.h
+++ b/src/ui/events/platform/wayland/wayland_event_watcher.h
@@ -9,6 +9,7 @@
 #include "base/memory/raw_ptr.h"
 #include "base/memory/weak_ptr.h"
 #include "base/message_loop/message_pump_for_ui.h"
+#include "base/synchronization/waitable_event.h"
 #include "base/threading/thread_checker.h"
 
 struct wl_display;
@@ -115,6 +116,10 @@
   // Used to verify watching the fd happens on a valid thread.
   THREAD_CHECKER(thread_checker_);
 
+  // Signalled when |this| is destroyed on the UI thread, before |thread_| is
+  // stopped. Declared before |thread_|, so it outlives the watching thread.
+  base::WaitableEvent ui_thread_gone_;
+
   // See the |use_threaded_polling_| and also the comment in the source
   // file for this header.
   std::unique_ptr<base::Thread> thread_;
